# Line endings and binary classification.
#
# Why this file exists: without it, how every text file in this repo is stored
# and checked out depends on each machine's `core.autocrlf`, which is a local
# setting nothing in the repo controls. Two checkouts with different values
# produce whole-file diffs against each other for reasons that have nothing to
# do with the content.
#
# `text=auto` pins the behaviour this repo already has: git normalises to LF in
# the object store and restores the platform's endings on checkout. Unity on
# Windows writes CRLF for scenes, ScriptableObjects and .meta, and every file in
# the working tree is CRLF today, so this is a no-op on the current tree - it
# just stops being a no-op that happens to be true by accident.
#
# Deliberately NOT `eol=lf`. Forcing LF into the working tree would flip every
# file once, Unity would rewrite them to CRLF on the next import, and the repo
# would churn forever. The one-time cost is not the problem; the permanent
# rewrite loop is.
#
# Note this does not explain the huge scene diffs. Those are real: a full
# SceneBuilder regeneration reassigns every fileID, which rewrites most lines of
# the YAML. See CLAUDE.md, "scenes are generated".
* text=auto

# Shell scripts are the one place eol=lf is mandatory rather than harmful. A
# CRLF checkout makes the shebang read as `/bin/sh\r`, and the script dies with
# "bad interpreter" naming an interpreter that does not exist. Nothing rewrites
# these files afterwards, so pinning them costs nothing - unlike the Unity YAML
# above, which Unity would immediately convert back.
tools/githooks/* text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Unity YAML. Explicit so a future `eol` rule can never catch them by accident.
*.unity   text
*.prefab  text
*.asset   text
*.meta    text
*.mat     text
*.anim    text
*.controller text

# Binaries: never diffed, never converted. Git guesses right on these today;
# saying so removes the guess.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.psd  binary
*.tga  binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.wav  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.ogg  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.fbx  binary
*.dll  binary
*.pdb  binary
*.unitypackage binary
